---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
import Legal from '../components/Legal.astro';
import Tbc from '../components/Tbc.astro';

// DRAFT FOR LEGAL REVIEW.
//
// The "no cookies" statement is not boilerplate, it was verified: the source
// contains no document.cookie, localStorage or sessionStorage use, no analytics
// or advertising snippet, and the built HTML requests no third-party origin
// (fonts are self-hosted). If that ever stops being true this page is wrong,
// and a consent banner becomes necessary rather than optional.
const title = 'Cookie Policy | Cellove Wellness';
const description =
  'This website sets no cookies and runs no analytics or advertising trackers. What that means, and what would change if it ever did.';
---
<Page title={title} description={description}>
  <PageHead
    eyebrow="Cookies"
    heading="Cookie Policy"
    body="The short version: this website does not set cookies and does not track you."
  />
  <Legal>
    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        <strong>This website sets no cookies.</strong> It runs no analytics, no advertising
        pixels, and no third-party scripts. Nothing is stored on your device, and we cannot
        tell who you are unless you choose to contact us.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>1. What cookies are</h2>
    <p>
      A cookie is a small file a website asks your browser to store, so it can recognise you
      on your next visit. Related technologies, such as local storage and tracking pixels,
      do much the same thing. They are useful for keeping you logged in, and they are also
      how most of the web follows people between sites.
    </p>

    <h2>2. What we use</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing. This is a brochure website with no login, no basket and no personalisation, so
      there is nothing for a cookie to remember. We decided not to install analytics rather
      than installing it and asking you to consent to it.
    </p>
    <p>Specifically, as at the date above, this site contains:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>no Google Analytics, Google Tag Manager or similar;</li>
      <li>no Meta, TikTok or LinkedIn advertising pixel;</li>
      <li>no session recording or heatmap tool;</li>
      <li>no embedded fonts, maps or videos loaded from another company&rsquo;s servers. Our typefaces are served from our own site, so no font provider is told you visited.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Because of this, there is no cookie banner. A banner that asks permission to do nothing
      would be noise.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Server logs</h2>
    <p>
      Our hosting provider, <Tbc what="hosting provider" />, keeps standard server logs
      recording requests to the site, which may include your IP address and browser type.
      These are not cookies, are not used to track you between sites, and are covered in our
      <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>4. When you leave this site</h2>
    <p>
      The WhatsApp and social media buttons are plain links. They set nothing while you are
      here. The moment you follow one, you are on a service run by another company, and that
      company&rsquo;s cookies and policies apply. We have no control over and no visibility of
      what they store.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Managing cookies anyway</h2>
    <p>
      Every major browser lets you see and delete stored cookies and block them by site,
      usually under privacy or site settings. Blocking cookies entirely will not affect
      anything on this website, since we do not use them.
    </p>

    <h2>6. If this changes</h2>
    <p>
      If we later add analytics, advertising or an online booking system, we will update this
      page, update the <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>, and ask for your consent before
      any non-essential cookie is set. Consent will be opt-in, refusing will be as easy as
      accepting, and nothing will be pre-ticked.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Questions about this page: <Tbc what="data protection contact name" /> at
      <Tbc what="data protection email" />.
    </p>
  </Legal>
</Page>
